WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:05.779
This is Peter Cooper from the NCBI.

00:00:05.779 --> 00:00:10.350
Today's webinar is about the RefSeq Functional
Elements project and Catherine Farrell, one

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:13.099
of the RefSeq people will be talking about
that.

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:17.420
And all the questions, even if we don't know
the answers now we will find them out and

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.070
put them in a Q&amp;A document.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:22.140
Okay I'm going to turn this over to Catherine.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.660
Hello, everyone.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:29.220
This is Catherine Farrell from the RefSeq
project . Here I am showing you the RefSeq

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.220
homepage.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:37.219
To talk specifically about the RefSeq Functional
Elements part of the project that is highlighted

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:50.839
here . before I do that I would like to remind
you of what the RefSeq scope has been so far

00:00:50.839 --> 00:00:57.070
we have been annotating genes which can either
be protein coding or non-coding.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:05.239
And the RefSeq accessions have prefixes like
NM_, or NP_s for proteins, or XM_s and XP_s

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.549
for model accessions that are protein coding.

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:11.640
NR_s or XR_s for non-coding transcripts.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:18.000
We have also been annotating Pseudogenes,
which can be transcribed or non-transcribed.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.830
The non-transcribed ones get NG_ accessions.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:29.180
We have also been annotating genomic regions
or segments with NG_ accessions.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:36.540
These have been typically regions containing
multiple genes or segments such as immumoglobulin

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.140
genes.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:43.670
We also have RefSeqGene representations for
clinically relevant gene regions.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.810
They are used as a foundation for variant
reporting.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:57.090
All of these are conventional gene type annotations
. however there are some points we need to

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.060
bear in mind.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:06.440
Only 1.1% of the human genome is protein coding.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:13.010
Less than 2% of the genome has conventional
gene type annotation when you include non-coding

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.520
genes and Pseudogenes genes along with protein
coding genes.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:25.520
It is also known from research, particularly
GWAS studies, that a lot of disease-associated

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.540
variation occurs in the non-coding genome.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:34.880
That leads us to the question of what other
functional content is present in the eukaryotic

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.590
genome?

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:44.290
There are gene regulatory elements such as
enhancers, silencers, promoters, TF binding

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:51.890
sites and locus control regions, such as this
example shown here.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:58.510
There are also structural elements including
boundary elements, matix attachment regions

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:02.599
and other structural regions associated with
chromatin confirmation.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:11.690
There are also elements involved in other
regulatory processes such as DNA recombination

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:16.480
or repair, DNA replication, or chromosome
maintenance or stability.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:23.800
It is known that variation in all of those
regions can result in disease.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.750
Also, higher order chromatin structure and
epigenetic modifications are important for

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.420
these functional processes.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:41.090
With that in mind we decided to annotate RefSeq
functional elements . The project overview

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:48.120
has this workflow where we would take known
elements that have been well-defined and experimentally

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:55.410
validated in the literature and data from
pubmed to curate the records, as well as data

00:03:55.410 --> 00:04:03.180
from databases such as the VISTA enhancer
database that has experimentally validated

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:10.160
enhancers and we would also use data from
collaborators to and enhance the experimentally

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.069
validated data when necessary.

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:17.599
The curated records would be of two types.

00:04:17.599 --> 00:04:25.680
One would be a record in the Gene database
which would contain metadata.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.550
I will demonstrate that later.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:35.870
We would also produce a RefSeq record in NCBI's
Nucleotide database.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:45.430
Those records are used to release FTP downloads
on NCBI's websites and they are used in NCBI

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.949
genome annotation and available on genome
browsers.

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:55.040
What is the scope of the RefSeq functional
element project ? First I would like to give

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:59.650
the definition of non-genic functional elements.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:05.690
That is any genomic element that has functional
significant based on experimental support

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:09.840
and is not otherwise considered to be a conventional
gene.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:17.190
The types that we represent include gene regulatory
elements such as enhancers, silencers, promoters

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:26.150
and structural elements known as boundary
elements, matrix attachment regions and other

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:32.240
elements of functional importance for example,
well-defined or clinically significant recombination

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.630
hotspots or DNA replication origins.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:43.860
Our annotation scope are for elements is that
elements have to be experimentally validated

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:51.240
in the literature, and we're only representing
human and mouse elements right now, with some

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:55.910
prioritization for elements implicated in
human disease.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:06:02.331
I also want to point out what RefSeq functional
elements are not going to represent, and that

00:06:02.331 --> 00:06:09.990
is, we are not representing predicted or imputed
elements from large-scale epigenetic studies

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:17.120
such as those covered in other resources such
as the ENCODE or NIH Roadmap Epigenomics projects

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:24.720
or the Ensembl Regulatory Build. in addition
the elements we represent are not as restrictive

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.050
as those in those other resources with respect
to element types.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:35.180
We can include element types that cannot be
readily predicted by epigenetic mapping studies

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.180
alone.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:43.930
An example would be recombination regions
or gene regulatory elements that are hard

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.660
to find by a particular experimental type
such as locus control regions.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:56.960
It is also important to remember that since
this is a new data set and we just had a initial

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:04.090
release that this data set is not yet complete,
so you may not find your favorite gene regulatory

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:07.630
element in the database at this point in time.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:13.880
Now I would like to describe the genomic RefSeqs
that we are making for these functinal elements

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:17.980
. these will have NG_ accession prefixes.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:26.280
They will span the genomic range of one or
more experimentally validated functional elements,

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:31.230
where we are grouping closely situated and
highly regulated elements together.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:36.970
For example, a cluster of enhancers that regulate
the same gene will be included on the same

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:46.300
NG accession . All of the accessions will
be associated with an NCBI BioProject accession

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:50.780
as shown here.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:55.080
You will identify those records on the flatfile
at the top.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:08:01.870
There will be a DBLINK to the bio project
accession.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:08.270
We will also include a parental region feature
on all of those records which will be marked

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:14.760
as a biological region such as this example
on the flatfile.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:20.270
All of these records will have a lot of feature
annotation.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:26.590
All features are in accordance with INSDC
specifications.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:35.110
Shown here is the INSDC website and their
feature table that defines and lists all the

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:38.440
definitions that they have.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:45.410
We are also using controlled vocabularies
from the Sequence Ontology when the INSDC

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:51.970
feature is not very descriptive . Such as
if it is just called a misc feature, -- miscellaneous

00:08:51.970 --> 00:09:01.950
feature, we will use Sequence Ontology terms
such as this example, conserved region.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:09.290
We also want to include experimental qualifiers
on other features to indicate the type of

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:12.430
experimental evidence that exists for the
feature.

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:19.770
We want to include at least one evidence call
from the Evidence and Conclusion Ontology.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:21.620
That is called a ECO ID.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.410
As shown in the highlighted example.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:32.930
We also want to include at least one citation
from PubMed, so users can click on the PMID

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:37.880
link and see the publication on those records.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:45.870
We are also using other INSDC qualifiers on
the feature annotation when applicable.

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:53.260
Such as feature class qualifiers and those
apply to both regulatory class features such

00:09:53.260 --> 00:10:00.680
as this example shown here or a recombination
class feature such as a meiotic recombination

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:03.520
hot spot example as shown here.

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:09.700
We also include note qualifiers which are
details from the literature as shown in this

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:19.720
example, and we also include db_xref qualifiers
to cross-reference database records.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:27.210
This example shows the cross-reference to
the gene ID for this particular RefSeq . There

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:35.740
will be other qualifiers for certain features
such as a bound moiety for protein bind features,

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:43.800
Or if a function is known for a particular
feature we can also indicate details for those.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:49.300
Now I will describe the Gene records that
we are representing.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:54.730
The Gene record is a central location with
integrated data from various resources.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:57.930
Here is an example of one such record.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:04.640
Here I would like to point out the top part
where we have a gene type called biological

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.930
region for all of these records.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:14.029
We are also going to display feature types
based on the feature annotation on the associated

00:11:14.029 --> 00:11:16.519
RefSeq.

00:11:16.519 --> 00:11:23.280
We also display other information such as
symbols and aliases from the literature as

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:31.350
well as a RefSeq summary that gives summary
of what the region is and what type of elements

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:34.110
are involved in that particular region.

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:44.089
We also show links to ortholog records, and
if there is one in the mouse ortholog.

00:11:44.089 --> 00:11:50.770
The gene record also contains other curated
areas, including a citations section with

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:57.830
a link to PubMed with related publications,
or Related sequences.

00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:04.400
The gene record also contains a feature annotation
graphic display.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:13.800
Right now the gene annotation is not showing
automatically by default because we only have

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:19.110
the annotation done in an NCBI interim annotation.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:24.779
I would like to draw your attention to the
Tracks button on the top right where you would

00:12:24.779 --> 00:12:33.829
need to turn on the Configure page and turn
on these particular tracks that are marked.

00:12:33.829 --> 00:12:39.430
And these show up on the browser like this.

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:45.940
In the biological regions track you would
see all of the feature annotations for the

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:49.260
particular region.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:55.790
Those features are color-coded according to
INSDC classifications.

00:12:55.790 --> 00:13:03.300
If you mouse over the title of one of those
features such as the locus control region

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:08.600
feature, you can bring a pop up box full of
information about that feature . that will

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:15.740
include all of the qualifiers previously described
such as the experiment qualifier with links

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:22.220
to PuMed IDs and you can also turn on the
links to sequence if you want to obtain that

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:25.970
sequence from there.

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:31.910
Here I am showing a least of feature keys
and feature classes and other controlled vocabularies

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:34.620
we have used so far.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:40.770
These are a list of features that have appeared
on the NCBI's human interim annotation.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:46.220
I want to point out that many of our features
are regulatory class features.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:50.579
We have a particular high amount of enhancer
features.

00:13:50.579 --> 00:13:55.430
We also have a lot of misc_recomb features.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:14:00.240
Most of those are either meiotic recombination
hotspots or they are non allelic homologous

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.930
recombination regions.

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:08.899
We also have several protein binding sites
that are experimentally validated in the literature.

00:14:08.899 --> 00:14:14.950
The current genome annotations status as mentioned
previously is based on interim annotations

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:23.290
and we have that data available in GFF3 format
on the FTP site as well as in NCBI graphical

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:27.420
view tracks as I showed two slides ago.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.990
We have that for both human and mouse.

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:35.920
Here is an example of our ftp site for the
human annotations.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:41.280
You need to go to that particular path to
find the file.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:47.660
And the highlighted file is the most recent
interim annotation.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:58.339
Our future plans are to do a full NCBI genome
annotation for human and mouse . we expect

00:14:58.339 --> 00:15:05.660
the human annotation to be released late this
year within the next month or so.

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:11.760
We continue to do more curation to produce
more functional element records.

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:19.050
We will provide links of gene targets for
particular elements or links of elements that

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:26.200
interact with each other in our gene records
. we will also provide links to Ensembl Regulatory

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:32.449
Build accessions that overlap RefSeq functional
element features . we are also working with

00:15:32.449 --> 00:15:37.389
USCSC to provide a UCSC Genome Browser track.

00:15:37.389 --> 00:15:45.540
More information is available on our website
. I would like to point out the data access

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:52.269
sections where you can find a lot more information
on how to access the data.

00:15:52.269 --> 00:16:00.839
You can access it via Gene, Nucleotide, BLAST,
BioProject and NCBI databases as well as the

00:16:00.839 --> 00:16:08.889
graphical displays and FTP sites . finally,
I would like to remind you to stay tuned to

00:16:08.889 --> 00:16:14.740
the NCBI Insights blog and NCBI social media
for future announcements about RefSeq Functional

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:16.520
Elements.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:19.610
Thank you for listening . if there are any
questions

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:23.910
Thanks very much Catherine, this is Peter
Cooper again.

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:29.440
We'll leave the questions pods open for a
few minutes if you have any questions please

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:30.440
type them in.

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:34.139
There do not seem to be any questions right
now.

00:16:34.139 --> 00:16:40.230
But if you have any questions about the material
we talked about today you can write to our

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:49.350
helpdesk . the address is, info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov
If you have any questions about the webinars

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:53.449
series, write to, webinars@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:16:53.449 --> 00:16:57.760
Thank you for coming.

00:16:57.760 --> 00:16:59.509
We're going to end the webinar now.

